\[ V = \pi r^2 h \]

where \( r \) is the radius and \( h \) is the height.

From the diagram, we can see that the radius \( r \) is 6 cm and the height \( h \) is 12 cm. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:

\[ V = \pi (6)^2 (12) \]
\[ V = \pi (36) (12) \]
\[ V = 432\pi \]

Since we are asked to find the volume of the half cylinder, we need to take half of this volume:

\[ V_{\text{half}} = \frac{432\pi}{2} \]
\[ V_{\text{half}} = 216\pi \]

Using the approximation \(\pi \approx 3.14159\), we can calculate:

\[ V_{\text{half}} \approx 216 \times 3.14159 \]
\[ V_{\text{half}} \approx 678.58464 \]

Rounding this to one decimal place, we get:

\[ V_{\text{half}} \approx 678.6 \]

Therefore, the volume of the half cylinder is \(\boxed{678.6}\).
