 To calculate the volume of the half cylinder, we need to use the formula for the volume of a cylinder and then take half of it. The formula for the volume of a full cylinder is:

\[ V = \pi r^2 h \]

where \( r \) is the radius and \( h \) is the height.

From the diagram, we can see that the radius \( r \) is 3 cm (half of the diameter, which is 6 cm) and the height \( h \) is 9 cm.

First, we calculate the volume of the full cylinder:

\[ V_{\text{full}} = \pi (3)^2 (9) \]
\[ V_{\text{full}} = \pi (9) (9) \]
\[ V_{\text{full}} = 81\pi \]

Now, we take half of this volume to get the volume of the half cylinder:

\[ V_{\text{half}} = \frac{1}{2} \times 81\pi \]
\[ V_{\text{half}} = 40.5\pi \]

Using the approximation \( \pi \approx 3.14159 \):

\[ V_{\text{half}} \approx 40.5 \times 3.14159 \]
\[ V_{\text{half}} \approx 127.2345 \]

Rounding to one decimal place, the volume of the half cylinder is approximately 127.2 cubic centimeters.

